<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <!--
          Transistorsoft background-geolocation licence — RELEASE builds only.

          The SDK is fully functional in DEBUG without a key, so this changes nothing about
          the debug flow we have been testing against. It is what stops a RELEASE build
          from refusing to run the location service.

          MECHANISM VERIFIED, not assumed. v9 keys are a new JWT format and v8 keys do not
          work with v9, so the registration method was worth checking rather than copying
          from memory: the constant "com.transistorsoft.locationmanager.license" is present
          in tslocationmanager-4.4.2.aar — the exact binary this build resolves — so the
          meta-data name is unchanged from v8 even though the key format is not.

          Not a secret. A licence key is bound to the application id and ships inside every
          APK by design; anyone can read it out of the package. It belongs in version
          control with the manifest it configures, and it is worthless against any other
          app id.

          Order 16956, product capacitor-background-geolocation, entitlement "core",
          app_id com.bigbulltech.b4c — matches applicationId in build.gradle.

          EXPIRY THAT BITES QUIETLY: max_build_stamp 20270828 with grace_builds 0. A
          release BUILT after 28 Aug 2027 will not validate. Builds made before then keep
          working indefinitely — this is a build-date limit, not a runtime kill switch —
          but there is no grace, so a renewal has to be in hand before that date or release
          builds simply stop.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.transistorsoft.locationmanager.license"
            android:value="eyJhbGciOiJFZERTQSIsImtpZCI6ImVkMjU1MTktbWFpbi12MSJ9..fhDfUCU1s6UDEc2cuHg1z4rodqP67odjYkPsrFZ58VasGS0evsqCzMZlYBkKbbktcy--jqkQ4avh7-y63QrHCA" />

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      Geofencing (Phase 2) — declared, not yet used.

      G4a installs the plugin and states what the app may ask for. NOTHING calls
      the plugin yet: no geofence is registered, no permission is requested at
      runtime, and no location is read. Declaring a permission is not the same as
      holding it — on Android 6+ every one of these is granted by the member at
      runtime, and until a later slice puts up the disclosure screen the app never
      asks. Installing this build changes no behaviour.

      COARSE alongside FINE because Android 12+ shows a single Precise/Approximate
      dialog and refuses to grant FINE unless COARSE is declared with it.

      BACKGROUND_LOCATION is the sensitive one, and the reason this feature needs a
      prominent-disclosure screen before the OS prompt and a written justification
      at store review. Android 11+ will not accept it in the same dialog as the
      others: it has to be a separate, later request, after when-in-use is already
      granted.

      FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION is required from API 34; we target 36. Without it
      the plugin's location service throws at startup on a modern device rather
      than failing quietly, so its absence would surface as a crash, not a
      degraded feature. The matching android:foregroundServiceType="location" is
      declared by the plugin's own AAR and arrives through manifest merge — we do
      not restate it here.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      REMOVED — permissions the geolocation AAR declares that we do not want.

      The plugin's library manifest asks for more than we need because it is built
      for continuous tracking. We run it in geofences-only mode (startGeofences):
      discrete arrive/depart events at one known station, never a breadcrumb trail.
      Manifest merge would hand us the tracking-mode permission surface anyway, so
      these two are removed deliberately rather than shipped by inheritance.

      ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION — "Physical Activity", a runtime permission with its own
      OS prompt. The SDK uses it to toggle location tracking on and off from
      detected motion, which is exactly the behaviour geofences-only mode does not
      use. Two reasons to refuse it:
        • Consent honesty. A member agreeing that the app notices they are at the
          station has not agreed to hand over what their body is doing. A prompt
          asking for physical activity is a different question than the one we
          asked on the disclosure screen, and it would arrive without explanation.
        • Play classification. Apps declaring ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION can be treated
          as health/fitness apps, pulling in review obligations that have nothing
          to do with a fire department roster.
      Documented cost of removal is that motion-triggered tracking needs 200–1000 m
      of movement to engage — a tracking-mode concern, not a geofence one.

      SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM — Android 14 restricted exact alarms and Play requires a
      justification for keeping them. The SDK uses them for stopTimeout,
      motionTriggerDelay and schedule: all tracking-mode config we do not set.
      Without it the SDK falls back to inexact alarms, which drift a few minutes.
      Nothing we do is minute-critical — a shift is measured in hours, and the
      server timestamps the row from the event, not from when the alarm woke up.

      BINDING FOLLOW-UP for the config slice: the plugin must be started with
        disableMotionActivityUpdates: true
      Removing the permission alone is only half the change — left unset, the SDK
      still tries to use a Motion API it no longer has rights to. The two belong
      together.

      ALSO NOTE for the handler slice: the one real consequence of dropping exact
      alarms is that getCurrentPosition() can fail outright while backgrounded. Our
      handlers must read coordinates from the geofence event itself and never call
      getCurrentPosition() from a background handler. The RPCs already take lat/lng
      as arguments for this reason.

      These are ASSERTIONS, not proofs: the docs never say geofences-only requires
      either permission, but they never say it works without them either. The field
      test is what settles it.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      The same capability under two more namespaces. Removing only the AOSP name
      looks complete and is not: the merged manifest carried all three.
        • com.google.android.gms — what Play Services used before Android 10 made
          ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION a first-class runtime permission. We ship minSdk 24,
          so this is the one that would actually apply on an older handset, and
          leaving it means the oldest devices we support keep the exact permission
          we just decided not to ask for.
        • com.huawei.hms — the Huawei Mobile Services equivalent, same capability.
      Play reads declared permissions across namespaces when classifying an app, so
      a stray gms declaration reintroduces the health/fitness signal on its own.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="com.google.android.gms.permission.ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="com.huawei.hms.permission.ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION" tools:node="remove" />
</manifest>
